@module dart-m02 | Dart Fundamentals
@summary Types, strings, numbers, booleans, operators, conversions and sound null safety.

@lesson dart-m02-l01 | Data types overview | 12
## Learning objectives
- List Dart's built-in types
- Understand the type hierarchy rooted at `Object?`
- Check and compare types at runtime

## Built-in types
Dart has special support for these types:

- Numbers: `int`, `double` (both subtypes of `num`)
- Text: `String`
- Truth values: `bool`
- Collections: `List`, `Set`, `Map`
- `Record` for grouping values, `Runes` for Unicode, `Symbol` (rare)
- The special value `null` (type `Null`)

```dart
void main() {
  int age = 30;
  double price = 9.99;
  num anyNumber = 7;      // can hold int or double
  String name = 'Ada';
  bool isMember = true;
  List<String> tags = ['dart', 'flutter'];
  Map<String, int> stock = {'apples': 12};
  (String, int) pair = ('score', 42); // a record

  print(age.runtimeType);   // int
  print(pair.$2);           // 42
}
```

## The type hierarchy
Every type in Dart is a subtype of `Object?`. Non-nullable types are subtypes of `Object`; `Null` sits beside them. Two other special types matter:

- `void`: "this value is not meant to be used" (functions that return nothing).
- `Never`: an expression that never completes normally, such as a function that always throws.

```diagram
                Object?
              /         \
          Object         Null
         /   |    \
      num  String  bool ...
     /   \
   int  double
```

## Checking types
Use `is` to test a type and `as` to cast. After a successful `is` check, Dart **promotes** the variable so you can use the type's members without casting:

```dart
void describe(Object value) {
  if (value is String) {
    print('Text with ${value.length} characters'); // promoted to String
  } else if (value is int) {
    print('Whole number, even: ${value.isEven}');
  } else {
    print('Something else: ${value.runtimeType}');
  }
}
```

> TIP: Prefer `is` checks with promotion over `as` casts. A wrong `as` cast throws at runtime; an `is` check just returns false.

:::check Is `int` a subtype of `num`?
Yes. Both `int` and `double` are subtypes of `num`.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Using `runtimeType` for logic. It is meant for debugging; use `is` checks instead.
- Assuming `num` and `int` are interchangeable everywhere: a `num` might be a `double`.

## Summary
- Built-in types: numbers, strings, booleans, collections, records, null.
- `Object?` is the top type; `Never` is the bottom type; `void` means "don't use".
- `is` checks types and promotes variables; `as` casts and can throw.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Built-in types
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Type system
