@lesson dart-m02-l02 | Strings | 14
## Learning objectives
- Create single-line, multi-line and raw strings
- Use common String methods
- Build strings efficiently with StringBuffer

## Creating strings
Dart strings are sequences of UTF-16 code units. You can use single or double quotes; the official style prefers single quotes.

```dart
void main() {
  final a = 'Single quotes';
  final b = "Double quotes – useful when the text contains 'apostrophes'";
  final c = 'It\'s escaped';
  final multi = '''
Line one
Line two
''';
  final raw = r'C:\new\folder – backslashes are literal';
  print(raw);
}
```

- Triple quotes (`'''` or `"""`) create multi-line strings.
- A leading `r` creates a **raw string** where backslashes are not escape characters, handy for file paths and regular expressions.
- Adjacent string literals are concatenated automatically: `'Hello ' 'world'`.

## Useful methods
Strings are **immutable**: every method returns a new string.

```dart
void main() {
  const title = '  Learn Flutter with Bimo  ';
  final clean = title.trim();
  print(clean.length);              // 23
  print(clean.toUpperCase());       // LEARN FLUTTER WITH BIMO
  print(clean.contains('Flutter')); // true
  print(clean.startsWith('Learn')); // true
  print(clean.indexOf('with'));     // 14
  print(clean.substring(6, 13));    // Flutter
  print(clean.replaceAll(' ', '-'));// Learn-Flutter-with-Bimo
  print(clean.split(' '));          // [Learn, Flutter, with, Bimo]
  print('ab' * 3);                  // ababab
  print('7'.padLeft(3, '0'));       // 007
}
```

## Comparing strings
Use `==` to compare contents. Dart compares string values, not references:

```dart
final input = 'yes';
print(input == 'yes');                       // true
print('Dart'.compareTo('Flutter') < 0);     // true: alphabetical order
print('DART'.toLowerCase() == 'dart');      // case-insensitive comparison
```

## StringBuffer for building text
Concatenating with `+` in a loop creates many temporary strings. `StringBuffer` collects pieces efficiently:

```dart
final buffer = StringBuffer();
for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  buffer.write('Item $i');
  if (i < 3) buffer.write(', ');
}
print(buffer.toString()); // Item 1, Item 2, Item 3
```

> TIP: For joining list items, `['a', 'b', 'c'].join(', ')` is even simpler.

:::check Does `name.toUpperCase()` change `name`?
No. Strings are immutable; the method returns a new string.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Expecting `trim()` or `replaceAll()` to modify the original string. Assign the result.
- Forgetting `r` before a regular expression and double-escaping every backslash.

## Exercise
Given `final email = '  Ada.Lovelace@Example.COM ';`, produce a normalised version that is trimmed and lowercase, then print the part before the `@`.
:::solution Example solution
```dart
void main() {
  final email = '  Ada.Lovelace@Example.COM ';
  final normalised = email.trim().toLowerCase();
  final user = normalised.split('@').first;
  print(normalised); // ada.lovelace@example.com
  print(user);       // ada.lovelace
}
```
:::

## Summary
- Use single quotes by convention; triple quotes for multi-line; `r''` for raw strings.
- Strings are immutable; methods return new strings.
- Compare with `==`; build large strings with `StringBuffer` or `join`.

## Further learning
- api.dart.dev ▸ dart:core ▸ String
