- isOdd()
+ isOdd
- odd
- % 2 property
> isOdd is a getter on int.

@lesson dart-m02-l04 | Booleans | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use `bool` values and logical operators
- Understand that Dart has no "truthy" values
- Write readable conditions

## true and false
The `bool` type has exactly two values: `true` and `false`. Unlike JavaScript or Python, Dart has **no truthiness**: an `if` statement requires an actual `bool`.

```dart
void main() {
  final items = <String>[];
  // if (items.length) {}        // Error: int is not a bool
  if (items.isEmpty) {
    print('Your cart is empty');
  }

  String? name;
  // if (name) {}                // Error: String? is not a bool
  if (name != null && name.isNotEmpty) {
    print('Hello $name');
  }
}
```

This strictness prevents subtle bugs such as treating `0` or an empty string as false by accident.

## Logical operators
```dart
final isLoggedIn = true;
final isAdmin = false;
print(isLoggedIn && isAdmin); // false – AND
print(isLoggedIn || isAdmin); // true  – OR
print(!isAdmin);              // true  – NOT
```

`&&` and `||` are **short-circuiting**: the right side is evaluated only when needed. That is why `name != null && name.isNotEmpty` is safe: if `name` is null, `name.isNotEmpty` never runs.

## Naming booleans
Good boolean names read like yes/no questions: `isVisible`, `hasError`, `canSubmit`, `shouldRetry`. Avoid negative names like `isNotReady`, because `!isNotReady` is hard to read.

```dart
final canSubmit = formIsValid && !isSubmitting;
if (canSubmit) submit();
```

Extracting a complex condition into a well-named boolean makes code self-documenting.

> TIP: Don't compare with `true`: write `if (isReady)` instead of `if (isReady == true)`. The exception is a nullable bool: `if (flag == true)` is a concise way to treat null as false.

:::check Is `if (0) {}` valid Dart?
No. Conditions must be of type bool; Dart has no truthy or falsy values.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Writing conditions with non-bool values out of habit from other languages.
- Using `&` or `|` (bitwise, not short-circuiting) instead of `&&` or `||`.

## Summary
- `bool` has two values, `true` and `false`; conditions must be bools.
- `&&`, `||` and `!` are the logical operators; `&&` and `||` short-circuit.
- Name booleans as questions and extract complex conditions.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Operators ▸ Logical operators
