@lesson dart-m02-l06 | Operators | 14
## Learning objectives
- Use arithmetic, equality, relational, assignment and type operators
- Apply null-aware and cascade operators
- Understand operator precedence

## Arithmetic and assignment
```dart
var x = 10;
x += 5;   // 15
x -= 3;   // 12
x *= 2;   // 24
x ~/= 5;  // 4
x++;      // 5 (postfix: returns old value, then increments)
++x;      // 6 (prefix: increments, then returns new value)
```

The difference between prefix and postfix matters when you use the result:

```dart
var a = 1;
print(a++); // prints 1, then a becomes 2
print(++a); // a becomes 3, prints 3
```

## Equality and relational operators
`==`, `!=`, `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`. In Dart, `==` calls the object's `==` method, so you can define equality for your own classes. To check whether two references point to the *same object*, use `identical(a, b)`.

## Type test operators
- `is`: true if the object has the type
- `is!`: true if it does not
- `as`: cast (throws if wrong)

## Null-aware operators
```dart
String? nickname;
print(nickname ?? 'Guest');   // ?? : use the right side if left is null
nickname ??= 'Ace';           // ??= : assign only if currently null
print(nickname?.length);      // ?. : call only if not null, else null
```

## Conditional expression
```dart
final age = 20;
final label = age >= 18 ? 'adult' : 'minor';
```

## Cascades
The cascade operator `..` performs several operations on the same object and returns the object itself:

```dart
final buffer = StringBuffer()
  ..write('Hello')
  ..write(', ')
  ..write('Dart');
print(buffer); // Hello, Dart

final list = <int>[]
  ..add(3)
  ..add(1)
  ..sort();
print(list); // [1, 3]
```

Use `?..` to start a cascade on a nullable object.

## Spread operators (preview)
`...` inserts all elements of a collection into another: `[0, ...list]`. Collections are covered in Module 5.

## Precedence
Multiplication binds tighter than addition, comparisons tighter than `&&`, and `&&` tighter than `||`. When in doubt, add parentheses:

```dart
final ok = (score > 70 && attempts < 3) || isTeacher;
```

> TIP: Parentheses cost nothing and make intent obvious. Use them whenever mixing `&&` and `||`.

:::check What does `x ??= 5` do when x is already 3?
Nothing. ??= assigns only when the variable is null, so x stays 3.
:::

## Common mistakes
- Confusing `=` (assignment) with `==` (comparison).
- Using `as` to "convert" values; it only casts, it does not convert (for example, `'5' as int` throws).

## Summary
- Compound assignments (`+=`, `~/=`) and increments (`++`) update variables.
- `is`, `is!`, `as` work with types; `identical` checks identity.
- `??`, `??=`, `?.` handle null concisely; `?:` chooses between values.
- `..` cascades operations on one object.

## Further learning
- dart.dev ▸ Language ▸ Operators
